Ladybird now gives a row only the specified block sizes of the
cells that span it alone, and takes a spanning cell’s specified
block size into its outer min-content block size, which the row
measures spread over the rows it spans. Blink does the same in
ComputeMinimumRowBlockSize(): A cell with an effective rowspan
of 1 adds its CSS block size to the row’s, and a spanning cell’s
goes into the minimum block size of its RowspanCell record, which
DistributeRowspanCellToRows() then spreads over the rows it spans.
WebKit’s RenderTableSection::calcRowLogicalHeight() leaves spanning
cells out of each row’s base height, and takes their extent into
account at the last row of their span. Gecko’s nsTableRowGroupFrame::
CalculateRowBSizes() sizes rows from cells with a rowspan of 1 first,
and then grows the spanned rows by what a spanning cell needs beyond
them. Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit 26.5 all give the rows
of the test 0px and 40px.
